#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180496} :activity/id #uuid "3e766c6c-8044-509d-9e8e-e4e4bbddcfe5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050773 :topic/id #uuid "659072cc-8b2c-5e8d-b6a6-88321581f47f" :topic/math-academy-id 3713} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179602 :question/id #uuid "6c8b136c-7944-599f-9399-19641212fcbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9537"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049147}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501169}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "3bb7385f-5c70-5eed-b8c4-e230a20a67a7" :step/math-academy-id 35728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180495}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179603 :question/id #uuid "16605c7c-c238-51ef-84e6-d5b5d58e1539" :question/math-academy-id "e-9539"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455447}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"} :step/id #uuid "8aff9a77-6b15-506d-8bc4-aca89e0ff920" :step/math-academy-id 35730 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180719 :tutorial/content "Given a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and a path $C$ defined by $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b],$ the line integral \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\ngives the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle from the initial point $\\mathbf{r}(a)$ to the terminal point $\\mathbf{r}(b)$ on the curve.\n\nNow suppose that $C$ is a *closed* curve. The **circulation** of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C,$ denoted using the Greek letter $\\Gamma$ (pronounced \"gamma\"), is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nRemember that the notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ simply reminds us that we're integrating along a closed path.\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ gives the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along the entire closed path. We can also think of $\\Gamma$ as a measure of how much $\\mathbf F$ circulates around $C.$\n\nLet's build some intuition by considering some examples. \n\n- Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the closed curve $C$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction, shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e9de4e3734be4f7d8f59f6d54d0ed92c51db3a0beb8277f4b450531b7dab332.png)\n\nIt's clear from the picture that $\\mathbf{F}$ *assists* the motion of a particle as it moves counterclockwise along the path. Therefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r >    0.\n$$\n\n- Now imagine that $C$ is traversed in the clockwise direction.\n\n![](images/16/16dd701d51e3b43714b58993f66e42359289f557ef86fd9091ab6b8bba7041e7.png)\n\nWe see that $\\mathbf F$ works *against* the particle's motion as it moves clockwise along the path. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, consider the vector field $\\mathbf{G}$ and the closed curve $C$ traversed in the clockwise direction, shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8bf269cecceeefc3e81b868a4fc4c54127f00432145782b24719bb49552868c7.png)\n\nSince the force $\\mathbf G$ acts perpendicular to the particle's motion as it moves around the path, it neither helps nor hinders its motion. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf G\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4446c197-397c-5e41-a7a9-22d57126ffae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5833 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2145b39b-47ee-5ae2-ad16-55a2719cc2e0" :step/math-academy-id 35732 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179605 :question/id #uuid "1e15ae93-0a63-506a-a0ae-b15e035f0494" :question/math-academy-id "e-9543"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049150}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415314}] :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "3bb98ffd-856f-5d48-bd6b-55f3885cdca8" :step/math-academy-id 35749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180721 :tutorial/content "The fundamental theorem of line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is a piecewise-smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and $f$ is differentiable function such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ \n\nNow, if $C$ is a closed path that's traversed once, then $\\mathbf r(a) = \\mathbf r(b),$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circulation of a conservative vector field around *any* closed path equals zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "5bc343b6-5187-5353-bb7b-23db12eaa417" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5836 :tutorial/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "345a9b9c-ae65-5c52-85e2-8ffa7905b813" :step/math-academy-id 35756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180499}}] :activity/title "Circulation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179865}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455518} :activity/id #uuid "129cb966-e09b-519a-a368-62dfaa636adb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050773 :topic/id #uuid "659072cc-8b2c-5e8d-b6a6-88321581f47f" :topic/math-academy-id 3713} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7781bfa2-360a-52f2-b8a7-5fd12172c6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455124} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad5e1c3-fe87-52c4-9343-a5e560de27a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455128} {:answer/id #uuid "9a55b0b7-ffd1-54c4-8119-7e1a1a488949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455185} {:answer/id #uuid "b6715a23-2351-5f62-8ba5-ef943e459d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455223} {:answer/id #uuid "c19b819d-c541-51b6-a6e0-7dae5f0106b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455223} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd93af99-5bab-55c2-8a85-e0d5efec7517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d78a254d-adb2-5551-a797-0a608524ca4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125669" :question/problem "![](images/36/36ebce64036b7661c81aa6525c407f698375544532985175cc465ddc3ff4f5a8.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/c4/c498ddeae8ba4325a4a1dfc0840520c4718bb50a0600970405374dde933e1b1d.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/74/740429a29b5eb4b72bbfae11e08c9ac272e72e31d395b4dad2602beb4d4ba40a.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "23ee9455-766f-5586-95a2-a9ed8dfe87ce"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7d45a6-b7b5-5b6e-a82c-a1748d83e603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454946} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4f36c3-2d0c-50c9-a36d-272fd914e84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455133} {:answer/id #uuid "c26f1bdf-26e0-5acd-bbc9-1a8e6be78f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455239} {:answer/id #uuid "c3275529-012a-5a31-82a4-2f28fcd1acb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455240} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6f64d6-184a-5394-8a28-a8c004572db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455240} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbf611c1-7a64-56ff-a8ce-599f171848cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "442b3fed-648b-5cc8-977b-6700ae26738b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125482" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "40211c38-7902-5a30-be18-54ba1e524e35" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40242699-9a76-5769-ad29-253e8f600b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455010} {:answer/id #uuid "63642342-0ea8-5389-ae31-2bce908c8ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455082} {:answer/id #uuid "90199dc9-7e02-5cd1-bdd7-df4867251c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455163} {:answer/id #uuid "97eeaf90-7d49-5a72-b321-ee76bbb6e4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455176} {:answer/id #uuid "c097de0b-9eac-5b4e-97f4-50838319facf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455176} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2afbdbe-c2b8-5f8b-8cd1-c52860a3b3d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "afd2a4b0-18e7-5bc4-9563-64bdaef16530" :question/math-academy-id "q-125666" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d042734a8c7483c54795cd6ec8527b43f398cb77487d054a36b27d704f27a318.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/f8/f8268167bb50f9d03cb38a3d505fa6b40003e307b149356089f5b0dc3dd32110.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a83934aa58040b4eb2bb957d1d12caba676e306e41086480d064935eab9f1c59.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "48918b6d-e2d1-57bd-906d-d8cb6cd2a9dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029ef420-9eaa-5c6d-8287-3ef33710e976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454907} {:answer/id #uuid "3af01276-3947-5c77-abb0-001b7f9268cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455000} {:answer/id #uuid "74a31f1c-47e3-545d-a815-1b80644ceb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455118} {:answer/id #uuid "c134f711-db33-5b9e-bc96-94b44710886e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455236} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1d9b86-ba57-557d-9af4-ca1029204f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455000} :answer-field/id #uuid "07b21c95-dc62-58a4-b763-c93eafdfda09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9318e5b-e833-584c-92bb-558dab7e94de" :question/math-academy-id "q-125640" :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1883ee3a3a99d43109bae2d2093aef35548dde9753cfb7dc19dfd77a104ec83.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/85/8595904122fac3431fc959b2fa4aa5a3062bd1bffd1a391a0861fa12f2498054.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1f31f2b6eefddedc46b8e845703904c0b4566963e64a644f7a4ab32bb7240c3.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "79347290-b94d-53e8-a6ae-104c05d725cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f5e806-242d-5b1a-8886-66c2da17be28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454981} {:answer/id #uuid "5b31d58a-98fe-5653-8b2e-f0931877d07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455066} {:answer/id #uuid "708e66ed-fb9c-572e-9876-d3e2f4e3957c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455113} {:answer/id #uuid "814fc88c-5be7-5618-be65-2f99b06af468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455137} {:answer/id #uuid "e34fc7a8-8d96-5fc5-a347-b84cc10b6757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455066} :answer-field/id #uuid "82c6016b-0343-53a1-a3ae-36d3278711ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "674f4764-9a63-51ae-b803-b7fdf438bb38" :question/math-academy-id "q-125667" :question/problem "![](images/13/13e4c6a06a44099024ce91ef6c9ed4679af037c2e794351597a4d604bf39dc6f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/63/6371bb04d33719ee18c65a92354e84be0e9077e780eac9559dcb7ec42deb8d99.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/0b/0bd9476fa9b19f04b40fbb9cca2986ed90495aa134307606622ef91070af19a7.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "fa7bb3f5-8a01-54f6-a7fb-3948ae86282d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455515}}] :activity/title "Circulation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454899}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548095} :activity/id #uuid "861ae291-f2e6-563c-be5b-6ff8635b285a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050773 :topic/id #uuid "659072cc-8b2c-5e8d-b6a6-88321581f47f" :topic/math-academy-id 3713} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f4c3d7-9a04-5668-b2e0-7bc01b46d0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415282} {:answer/id #uuid "50325077-b1b2-55cc-a916-71cd3fe9281a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415288} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f96501-879d-531b-beaa-b191c153f318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415297} {:answer/id #uuid "afd3784e-7e64-5098-b432-0a060374a546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415298} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb55bf7-4c03-598b-b1a4-e6045754359b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415306}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415288} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e78bf84-51cb-53d9-8508-33548a10f308" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b79e3fb3-3865-5932-ba2e-8b5992286bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125481" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=-y\\,i+x\\,j$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =-y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =5{sin}^{2}⁡t+5{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "24f80556-3fb3-5fdf-92b1-a1e2e560a803" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548165}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "590a9072-3394-532b-a5a0-dc5f49b25f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257555} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4a844c-c7fa-5967-8a71-4a23cafb35dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257609} {:answer/id #uuid "878f3872-5ea2-514f-b1fd-e429a62b2e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257968} {:answer/id #uuid "a47316a6-f4ef-5c35-b6df-f442c5ac4c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258218} {:answer/id #uuid "f7994ada-b57a-50c5-8da3-ca283cecf0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258218} :answer-field/id #uuid "6199c37f-2024-560d-965e-527c92de8df5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c33250ca-91bb-5b15-bb01-0f4acbe13913" :question/math-academy-id "q-125451" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\sqrt{3}{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\sqrt{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "38bc239e-ca3a-5ecd-964e-520d3c510b6f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548181}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ccf9fe-9fff-57a9-9304-9ed3ab411a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470949} {:answer/id #uuid "cd38aae3-eb89-502e-95f5-f0b67c9bface" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470949} :answer-field/id #uuid "b676af05-3dc2-57c5-937a-36acc27a6987" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471980} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4d25790-233a-5252-8654-1ee93a945a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470951} {:answer/id #uuid "88bef363-90dd-5f58-8f3e-0e6fa9a731c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470951} :answer-field/id #uuid "b508976c-ce23-5a0b-8ee8-1332aba82f50" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471981} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f1634ca-676c-56be-805f-f8a4da6ba9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470953} {:answer/id #uuid "2d74d261-31ed-592a-8886-2e31a70f20d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470953} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a1e2d7d-bec5-5fe7-8248-ae4746a2c58c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d4a2162-ef8c-55f6-9a59-3237823f3b0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125660" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({e}^{x+y}-4x)\\,i+({e}^{x+y}+6y)\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $F$ is **not** conservative\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0$ for some closed paths $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\n- Statement III is false. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "c689e440-61b7-5e06-a4ee-627de5062753"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548165 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5d80f62-7611-5589-bb35-f2bd8dd2ac54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448188} {:answer/id #uuid "ceba6fca-85bb-5f5b-b472-da4e26311629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448189} :answer-field/id #uuid "c16dc663-73ea-51dc-bd73-3fce8f2608a4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449134} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d66252c-0a79-586a-9a03-83c0f08ea4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448190} {:answer/id #uuid "068689c6-ba01-5b3f-8861-81453ed7f373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448190} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c337a6b-db58-56f1-8586-a438e958e686" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449135} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0583a8b5-3c5d-55d5-9ab8-38e4dbed7c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448192} {:answer/id #uuid "0e840e72-c567-58eb-8d86-b77eb69e1aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448193}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448193} :answer-field/id #uuid "94fc3bec-7574-5b7d-9315-47b094cd5a5b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7b7ddac2-e3bd-590b-a396-56e8108da7f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-125647" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $∇f$ is conservative\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ca68ab66-0db0-5b63-b248-8566a4a54391" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548181 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3484a3-4e24-56fb-b7a3-9ddea884c66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415285} {:answer/id #uuid "5f428d96-a179-52b0-a209-2ee6f08dffb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415291} {:answer/id #uuid "d9418009-409d-5c05-bc92-ce23127789ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415299} {:answer/id #uuid "e65b56f6-b686-5dc3-9782-652e4c386b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415302} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8f2775-c0fe-5dbb-b5a2-d9f8ae009d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415304}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415304} :answer-field/id #uuid "8daab46c-8511-5579-956f-63a995a70f85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4ec2acb3-1493-5600-bce3-9a9694ba6ec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125470" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb776051-0df8-591b-b4b4-5bd5f81018bb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548091}}] :activity/title "Circulation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547301}]}
